<BaseLayout title="Medical claim redesign — Dana" description="A guided, responsive, and accessible medical claim experience for Canada Life plan members.">
<header class="case-hero"><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Canada Life · Released product</p><h1>Redesigning the post-visit medical claim experience</h1><p class="lead">A guided, responsive, and accessible reimbursement flow for plan members submitting medical expenses and supporting documents.</p></div></header>
<section class="snapshot"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project snapshot</p><h2>Modernizing a high-use benefits journey</h2><p>The experience was redesigned during a broader migration from SAP Hybris to Salesforce Industries. It had to simplify claim submission while supporting complex benefit rules, responsive layouts, technical constraints, and accessibility requirements.</p></div><dl><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>UX Designer, end-to-end product experience</dd></div><div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Group-benefits members requesting reimbursement after receiving care</dd></div><div><dt>Platforms</dt><dd>Responsive web and mobile</dd></div><div><dt>Partners</dt><dd>Product, business analysis, research, content, accessibility, QA, and engineering</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Released within a platform serving 10M+ users</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Members needed clarity at every step</h2><p class="large">After paying for an eligible medical service, members needed to provide practitioner information, expense details, and supporting documents to request reimbursement. The redesigned experience needed to reduce cognitive effort while helping members understand what was required before submission.</p><div class="challenge"><strong>Design challenge</strong><p>How might we create a clearer claim journey while supporting multiple claim types, platform migration, responsive behavior, and accessibility requirements?</p></div></div></section>
<section class="tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Design approach</p><h2>Three decisions shaped the experience</h2><div class="decisions"><article><span>01</span><h3>Turn a dense form into a guided journey</h3><p>The flow focused members on one group of related information at a time, without exposing the full complexity at once.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Explain documents at the point of need</h3><p>Receipt and supporting-document guidance appeared near the relevant action rather than on a distant instruction page.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Prevent errors before submission</h3><p>Required fields, validation, error communication, and confirmation states helped members resolve issues and understand next steps.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Experience model</p><h2>A clearer path from preparation to confirmation</h2><ol class="flow"><li><b>1</b><strong>Prepare</strong><small>Understand required information</small></li><li><b>2</b><strong>Describe</strong><small>Enter provider and expense details</small></li><li><b>3</b><strong>Document</strong><small>Add receipts and supporting files</small></li><li><b>4</b><strong>Review</strong><small>Confirm information</small></li><li><b>5</b><strong>Submit</strong><small>Receive next steps</small></li></ol><p class="note">Product screens and annotated before-and-after flows will be added after a confidentiality review.</p></div></section>
<section class="dark"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Accessibility and delivery</p><h2>Designed through implementation—not only handoff</h2></div><div><p>I collaborated with engineering through design reviews and worked with accessibility, QA, and product partners as the experience moved toward release. The work accounted for clear labels, understandable validation, keyboard and focus behavior, readable hierarchy, and responsive use.</p><p>Post-launch product metrics were not available to the design team, so this case study does not claim unsupported improvements. Success was reflected in release readiness, accessibility review, and production acceptance.</p></div></div></section>
<section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Reflection</p><h2>Good enterprise design makes complexity manageable</h2><p class="large">Simplifying an experience does not mean removing the system’s complexity. It means deciding when users encounter it, explaining it clearly, and working with technical partners so the intended experience survives implementation.</p><a class="button" href="/#work">Return to selected work</a></div></section>
